<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sicily</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sicily is the largest island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, off the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Greek cities fought over it for centuries, and its loss to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> was a major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>. It was later ruled by Muslim dynasties, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> note, and its invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1943 hastened the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sicily.jpg" alt="Sicily" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sicily_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The learning of the Abbasid Golden Age was recovered through Arabic translations in <b>Sicily</b> and Spain, and later European science was built in part upon it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture and the trans-Saharan trade were further developed under their rule, bringing economic expansion and a growing urban population, and the campaigns in <b>Sicily</b> that began in 827 brought new revenues to the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Attica could not feed itself, so the harbour of Piraeus, laid out as a planned town in the fifth century, imported grain from the Black Sea, Egypt and <b>Sicily</b>, and securing that supply shaped Athenian policy for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 412 BCE, after the Athenian disaster in <b>Sicily</b>, Chios revolted with Spartan support; the Athenians landed, defeated the Chians in several battles and ravaged the countryside, and the island served as a Spartan base until 404 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sicily</b> and Sardinia supplied grain and silver, and Majorcan cartographers produced works such as the Catalan Atlas of 1375.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hauteville brothers Robert Guiscard and Roger I conquered southern Italy and <b>Sicily</b> between the 1040s and 1091, Roger II was crowned King of Sicily in 1130, and Bohemond of Taranto founded the Principality of Antioch during the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
